    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A not so common consensus is derived from studies investigating the serotonin transporter gene (5-HTTLPR) and its association with aggression; however, Suzuki et al. (2011) found no significant differences in aggression or emotionality related to haplotypes in a diverse Japanese population. Regarding social cohesion, variations in the oxytocin receptor gene (OXTR) had been linked to differences in these features, but findings across different populations are mixed. Research by Kirschbaum et al. (2009) indicates that saliva samples had a sensitivity of 61% and a specificity of 95% in relation to detecting serotonin transporter (5HTT) expression, implying a certain degree of genetic variance. Still, the literature requires more definitive studies specifically tying these markers to certain key ancestral groups consistently but suggests a complex interplay between genetic predispositions and cultural factors. Research paying closer attention to these details is needed to provide a clearer picture.",
      "source": "target",
